From c78bc654eb6bbfe9c88f247271408ed3c9ee7284 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:19:56 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 01/23] [mya] Stop building MYA_HEAP configurations with RTTI
 and default symbol visibility Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

Build flags only (plus the one test that needed them).

MYA_HEAP builds currently turn on -frtti and turn off -fvisibility=hidden /
GCC_SYMBOLS_PRIVATE_EXTERN (and TAPI) for every WebKit project, so that mya
could name an object's dynamic type through its std::type_info and the
type_infos of base classes resolved across images. The heap walk now reads
types from DWARF through liblldb instead: an object's dynamic type comes from
the destructor in its vtable, whose linkage name the debug info has (see the
debug info patch later in this series). Nothing needs a type_info any more.

Why take it:
- The MYA_HEAP configuration (Debug, macOS) goes back to compiling and linking
  the way every other Debug build does: no RTTI, hidden visibility, TAPI on.
  Debug MYA_HEAP builds and ordinary Debug builds then differ only in debug
  info, which is what mya actually reads.
- Exporting every symbol made images bigger, link slower, and hid ODR and
  export-list mistakes that a production build would catch.

What we lose if it is not taken: nothing functional in the rest of this
series; it only keeps paying the RTTI/visibility cost in MYA_HEAP builds.

TypeinfoTest.cpp, the test that checked RTTI and liblldb agree, goes with it;
the DebugInfo suite added later replaces it.

Note: this reverts the RTTI and visibility flags the prototype stopped using.
WebCore, PAL, WebGPU, WebKit, ANGLE and libwebrtc still have
MYA_HEAP-conditional GCC_SYMBOLS_PRIVATE_EXTERN / TAPI / export settings from
the earlier patch; whether those can go too should be checked separately.

